01-10-06

OPCD_3.0           

The tar file OPCD_3.0.tar
*************************
The tar file OPCD_3.0.tar contains data and codes to compute Opacity Project (OP) 
mean opacities and radiative accelerations. It also includes the software for
both the local and remote versions of the OPserver. Full documentation is given in 
the files OPCD.ps (OPCD.pdf) and OPserver.ps (OPserver.pdf).

Extracting from tar file
************************
Entering 

tar -xvf OPCD_3.0.tar 

creates a directory OPCD_3.0 with sub-directories mono, codes, testin, testout, 
opserver and opserver_OSC. The main data files, fmzz.ttt.gz and fazz.ttt.gz, are 
formatted and gzipped.

Installation of OP codes and data
*********************************
The installation enables the compilation of the OP codes and the creation of 
binary data files used by these codes. Three methods of installation are available.
  
(1) Makefile procedure provided in the directory OPCD_3.0. Please enter 
    ./configure
    make
    make install
The configure script writes the file Makefile.incl listing the active FORTRAN 
and C compilers and the corresponding compiler and library options. This file is then 
read by the makefile procedure. The installer may change the selected compiler by 
editing this file. After installation, test-runs can be performed for all codes with 
the command
    make test
or alternatively, for each individual code, with the command
    make code_name
where code_name can be one of the following: accfit, accv, ax, mixv, mixz, mx, mxz 
or opfit. The test-runs write output files in the directory OPCD_3.0 which should
be comparable with the standard files provided in the subdirectory testout.

(2) Bash script BASHOP provided in directory OPCD_3.0. Please enter
    ./BASHOP
and then, when prompted, enter the name of the FORTRAN compiler to be used.  A set of 
tests are performed after installation to which the installer must enter "Y" when 
prompted.

(3) Manual installation. Details of procedure to be used are described in the document
OPCD.ps in directory OPCD_3.0.

The installation creates binary files mzz.ttt and azz.ttt in subdirectory mono. 
The files fmzz.ttt.gz and fazz.ttt.gz are retained, but to save disk overhead, they
can be deleted after installation with the command
    ./CLEANOP
or alternatively
    make clean
The installation creates in the directory OPCD_3.0 the executable "*.out"
files for the OP codes mixv, opfit, mx, mxz, mxz, mixz, accv, accfit and ax.


Installation of the OPserver
****************************

(1) Local version
*****************
This version requires beforehand the installation of the OP codes and data as 
described above. Also, OPserver must be compiled with a FORTRAN-90 compiler. It 
is installed with the command
    make opserver
A test run of all the subroutines can be performed with the command
    make test_opserver
which produces outputs comparable with those in the subdirectory testout.

(2) Remote version
******************
This version of the OPserver acceses the OP data at the Ohio Supercomputer Center, 
and therefore the installation of the OP data is not necessary. It requires a 
FORTRAN-90 compiler, the GNU gcc compiler and the libstdc++ and libcurl
libraries. Most modern linux installations include this software. Installation
of the libcurl libraries can be verified with the command
    curl-config
OPserver is installed with the commands
    ./configure
    make
    make opserver_OSC
A test run of all the subroutines can be performed with the command
    make test_opserver_OSC
which produces outputs comparable with those in the directory testout.
